  <title>
    <media type="image" width="40px" height="40px" its:translate="no" src="figures/orca-logo.svg" /> Starting and stopping Orca
  </title>
  <section id="starting-orca">
    <title>Starting Orca</title>
    <p>
      Use your desktop's accessibility settings to enable its screen reader or arrange for Orca
      to start automatically. In GNOME, the default shortcut to turn the screen reader on or off
      is <keyseq>
        <key>Alt</key>
        <key>Super</key>
        <key>S</key>
      </keyseq>.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can also start Orca by running <code>orca</code> in a terminal or your desktop's
      Run dialog.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="stopping-orca">
    <title>Stopping Orca</title>
    <p>
      If your desktop manages Orca as a service, use its screen reader controls to turn Orca
      off. The quit command is for an Orca instance started as a regular application. Using it
      for a systemd-managed instance can cause the service to restart Orca.
    </p>
    <section id="commands-stopping-orca">
      <title>Commands</title>
      <p>
        <em style="strong">Preferences location:</em>
        <gui>Commands</gui> category, <gui>Commands</gui> section, <gui>Screen Reader Management</gui> group.
      </p>
      <list>
        <item>
          <p>
            <gui>Quit the screen reader application</gui>
            Unbound by default.
          </p>
        </item>
      </list>
    </section>
  </section>
  <section id="command-line-options">
    <title>Command-line options</title>
    <p>
      These commands display information or manage extension approval without starting Orca:
    </p>
    <list>
      <item>
        <p>
          <code>orca --help</code>: Display the available
          options and their usage.
        </p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>
          <code>orca --version</code>: Display the
          installed version of Orca.
        </p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>
          <code>orca -l</code> or <code>orca --list-apps</code>: List known running
          applications.
        </p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>
          <code>orca --approve-extension FILENAME</code>: Approve a user
          extension for loading.
        </p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>
          <code>orca --revoke-extension FILENAME</code>: Revoke a user
          extension's approval.
        </p>
      </item>
    </list>
    <p>
      These commands start Orca:
    </p>
    <list>
      <item>
        <p>
          <code>orca -p NAME</code> or <code>orca --profile NAME</code>: Start Orca with
          the named settings profile.
        </p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>
          <code>orca -s</code> or <code>orca --setup</code>: Start Orca and open
          the Preferences window. If Orca is already running, open its Preferences window
          instead.
        </p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>
          <code>orca --speech-system NAME</code>: Start Orca with
          the selected speech system. The supported names are <code>speechdispatcherfactory</code> for Speech
          Dispatcher (the default) and <code>spiel</code> for Spiel. The selected
          backend must be installed and available.
        </p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>
          <code>orca -r</code> or <code>orca --replace</code>: Start Orca,
          replacing an instance started as an application.
        </p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>
          <code>orca --debug</code>: Start Orca with
          verbose debugging enabled, saving the log to a file with a timestamped filename.
        </p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>
          <code>orca --debug-file FILE</code>: Start Orca with
          verbose debugging enabled, saving the log to the specified file.
        </p>
      </item>
    </list>
    <p>
      If Orca is already running as an application, add <code>-r</code> or <code>--replace</code> when starting it with
      debugging, a different speech system, or a different profile. For example, <code>orca --replace --debug</code>. If your desktop
      manages Orca as a service, use the desktop's screen reader controls to stop it before
      starting Orca with these options, rather than using <code>--replace</code>.
    </p>
  </section>
